//? Title: Canopy Clustering: Actual Overlap, Candidate Pairs and Limits
//? Subtitle: Checked Euclidean construction, native iris data and the original ninety-point lattice
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Large clustering tasks can require many costly pairwise comparisons. We seek
overlapping candidate groups that reduce those comparisons while retaining useful
neighbours. Canopy clustering builds such groups. We inspect actual distance decisions on four points,
measure candidate-pair coverage on native iris data, and retain the original
ninety-point experiment and its threshold sweep. Its points are translated grids,
not Gaussian draws. Native C++ now supplies checked construction traces, inverted
memberships, unique shared pairs and the reusable grid generator. A four-point
counterexample corrects the old claim that loose radius at least tight radius
always preserves close pairs. We derive a sufficient metric condition instead.
The implementation uses full Euclidean scans and performs no downstream clustering;
counts here do not establish acceleration or unchanged clustering accuracy.
)

\toc

> Four points: consumed seeds can still join another canopy

Take coordinates 0,1,2,3, loose radius T1=2.5 and tight radius T2=1.5.
Point zero seeds the first canopy: members are original ids [0,1,2], while
ids [0,1] become ineligible to seed again. Point two is the next eligible
seed. Its canopy includes all four points, including already consumed ids
zero and one. Consuming a point affects seeding, not later membership searches.
Both radius tests are strict; equality is outside. The trace records the actual
distance evaluations and eligibility transitions in input-index order.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/canopy_steps.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::canopy_clustering_trace({{0},{1},{2},{3}},2.5,1.5);
  const auto coverage=dm::algorithms::canopy_coverage(4,trace.canopies);
  if(trace.canopies.size()!=2 || coverage.total_memberships!=7 || coverage.shared_pairs.size()!=6) return 1;
  std::print("| canopy | seed | point | distance | already ineligible | member | distance < T2 | newly consumed |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& visit:trace.visits)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.1f} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",visit.canopy,trace.canopies[visit.canopy].center,
        visit.point,visit.distance,visit.already_removed,visit.member,visit.within_tight,visit.newly_removed);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.width=6;node.height=3.2;node.font_size=19;
  for(std::size_t id=0;id<trace.canopies.size();++id) {
    const auto& c=trace.canopies[id];const auto& step=trace.steps[id];
    node.fill=id?dm::RGB{220,252,231}:dm::RGB{239,246,255};
    diagram.node(std::format("step{}",id),{static_cast<double>(id)*9,0},
        std::format("canopy {}: seed {}\nmembers {}\nnewly consumed {}\neligible seeds: {} -> {}",
            id,c.center,c.members,step.newly_removed,step.remaining_before,step.remaining_after),node);
  }
  diagram.edge("step0","step1","next eligible")
      .text({4.5,2.8},"coordinates [0, 1, 2, 3]; loose radius 2.5; tight radius 1.5",20)
      .text({4.5,-2.7},"all original points remain available for membership tests",19)
      .save_svg("build/canopy_steps.svg");
  std::print("\nActual distance evaluations: {}; coordinate differences: {}.\n",trace.distance_evaluations,trace.coordinate_differences);
  std::print("Memberships: {}; overlapping points: {}; within-canopy pair visits: {}; unique shared pairs: {}.\n",
      coverage.total_memberships,coverage.overlapping_points,coverage.pair_visits,coverage.shared_pairs.size());
}
```
