| 0 | 0 | 1 | 1.0 | false | true | true | true |
| 0 | 0 | 2 | 2.0 | false | true | false | false |
| 0 | 0 | 3 | 3.0 | false | false | false | false |
| 1 | 2 | 0 | 2.0 | true | true | false | false |
| 1 | 2 | 1 | 1.0 | true | true | true | false |
| 1 | 2 | 2 | 0.0 | false | true | true | true |
| 1 | 2 | 3 | 1.0 | false | true | true | true |

Actual distance evaluations: 8; coordinate differences: 8.
Memberships: 7; overlapping points: 3; within-canopy pair visits: 9; unique shared pairs: 6.
\image(figures/canopy_steps.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Two actual seed-pool transitions and eight actual distance checks. Three points overlap, and nine within-canopy pair visits represent only six unique pairs.)
\alttext(A first canopy seeded by point zero contains ids zero, one and two and consumes zero and one. The next canopy, seeded by point two, contains every point and consumes two and three.)

The lowest eligible index is always selected. Every step consumes its seed,
so construction ends with at most n canopies, including when T2=0. Every point
is covered: a tight-radius removal implies loose-radius membership because
T2<=T1; a point not removed earlier eventually seeds its own canopy, where
distance zero is below positive T1. Coverage of individual points does not
prove coverage of every close pair.

The original canopy method uses a cheap first-stage proximity measure before
more expensive clustering. Its author describes accuracy preservation as conditional
on properties of that measure, not unconditional. Our implementation is a deterministic
all-original-points Euclidean variant, with no approximate distance or spatial index.
See the [author's description](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~reinf/web/talks-2001/01-03-19.mccallum.html).

> Iris: count retained comparisons on native data

The native iris frame has 150 flowers and four measurements in centimetres.
We use all four coordinates directly; no species labels, fitting or downstream
clusterer enter this calculation. The radius therefore depends on measurement
scale and this chosen Euclidean representation. The original dataset order also
matters because it controls seed choice. Its species grouping is not shuffled.
The [R dataset documentation](https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/datasets/html/iris.html)
describes the measurements and ordering.

Fix T2=0.6 and increase T1. The seed decisions depend only on T2 and the
input order, so the centres stay fixed. Loose-radius memberships, and hence the
set of unique candidate pairs, can only grow. Out of 150*149/2=11,175 possible
unordered pairs, the table counts those sharing at least one canopy. Repeated
visits to a pair in several canopies do not count as additional retained pairs.
This diagnostic explicitly enumerates candidates; it is not a speed benchmark.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/canopy_iris.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();
  const std::vector<std::string> columns{"Sepal.Length","Sepal.Width","Petal.Length","Petal.Width"};
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> points(iris.nrows());
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<iris.nrows();++i) for(const auto& column:columns) points[i].push_back(iris.double_at(column,i));
  std::vector<std::size_t> initial_seeds;
  std::vector<double> radii,pair_fraction,overlap_fraction;
  std::print("| T1 | seeds | memberships | overlapping points | distance checks | pair visits | unique candidate pairs / 11175 | candidate fraction |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(double loose:{.6,.9,1.2,1.8,2.4}) {
    const auto trace=dm::algorithms::canopy_clustering_trace(points,loose,.6);
    const auto coverage=dm::algorithms::canopy_coverage(points.size(),trace.canopies);
    std::vector<std::size_t> seeds;for(const auto& canopy:trace.canopies) seeds.push_back(canopy.center);
    if(initial_seeds.empty()) initial_seeds=seeds;
    if(seeds!=initial_seeds || coverage.uncovered_points || trace.distance_evaluations!=150*seeds.size()) return 1;
    radii.push_back(loose);pair_fraction.push_back(static_cast<double>(coverage.shared_pairs.size())/11175);
    overlap_fraction.push_back(static_cast<double>(coverage.overlapping_points)/150);
    std::print("| {:.1f} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.6f} |\n",loose,seeds.size(),coverage.total_memberships,
        coverage.overlapping_points,trace.distance_evaluations,coverage.pair_visits,coverage.shared_pairs.size(),pair_fraction.back());
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().line(radii,pair_fraction,"candidate fraction",{37,99,235},2.5)
      .points(radii,pair_fraction,"",{37,99,235},4).line(radii,overlap_fraction,"overlap fraction",{220,38,38},2.5)
      .points(radii,overlap_fraction,"",{220,38,38},4).title("Iris: loosen membership, keep the same seeds")
      .x_label("loose Euclidean radius T1 (T2 = 0.6)").y_label("fraction").y_limits(0,1.17)
      .save_svg("build/canopy_iris.svg");
}
```
