| T1 | seeds | memberships | overlapping points | distance checks | pair visits | unique candidate pairs / 11175 | candidate fraction |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.6 | 26 | 256 | 85 | 3900 | 1904 | 1544 | 0.138166 |
| 0.9 | 26 | 645 | 144 | 3900 | 9771 | 3860 | 0.345414 |
| 1.2 | 26 | 1029 | 149 | 3900 | 23223 | 4941 | 0.442148 |
| 1.8 | 26 | 1607 | 150 | 3900 | 53717 | 5980 | 0.535123 |
| 2.4 | 26 | 2024 | 150 | 3900 | 82582 | 8752 | 0.783177 |
\image(figures/canopy_iris.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Unique retained-pair fraction and overlapping-point fraction as T1 increases on iris. These quantities measure different aspects of overlap.)
\alttext(Two curves show the fraction of possible pairs sharing a canopy and the fraction of iris flowers belonging to more than one canopy. The table prints actual counts.)

A reduced candidate count is useful only if it retains pairs needed by the later
method. No second, expensive metric or final clustering accuracy is measured here.
The current full-scan construction may itself cost more than a suitable index;
we make no claim that this small dataset is accelerated by the diagnostic.

> Preserve the original ninety-point experiment

The original code places thirty points on a six-by-five rectangular grid about
each of (0,0), (10,0) and (5,8), with offsets -0.15 and -0.1 and spacings
0.05 and 0.04. These are deterministic translated lattices. The reusable
translated_grid_2d() API now supplies those exact coordinates in the original
order. Actual seeds are the first grid points, at the lower corners, rather
than the nominal translation centres.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/canopy_clusters.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto points=dm::datasets::translated_grid_2d({{0,0},{10,0},{5,8}},6,5,.05,.04,-.15,-.1);
  const auto canopies=dm::algorithms::canopy_clustering(points,3,2.5);
  const auto ownership=dm::algorithms::canopy_coverage(points.size(),canopies);
  if(points.size()!=90 || canopies.size()!=3 || ownership.overlapping_points) return 1;
  const std::vector<dm::RGB> colors{{239,68,68},{37,99,235},{22,163,74}};
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for(std::size_t id=0;id<canopies.size();++id) {
    std::vector<double> x,y;
    for(std::size_t p=0;p<points.size();++p) if(ownership.point_canopies[p].front()==id) {x.push_back(points[p][0]);y.push_back(points[p][1]);}
    plot.points(x,y,std::format("canopy {}: {} points",id,x.size()),colors[id],3);
  }
  const auto centres=dm::algorithms::canopy_centers(points,canopies);
  std::vector<double> cx,cy;for(const auto& centre:centres) {cx.push_back(centre[0]);cy.push_back(centre[1]);}
  plot.points(cx,cy,"actual seeds",{17,24,39},6).title("Original 90-point lattice: disjoint membership at T1=3")
      .x_label("x").y_label("y").y_limits(-1,11).save_svg("build/canopy_clusters.svg");
  std::print("| T1 | actual seed ids | memberships | distance checks | coordinate differences | pair visits | unique pairs / 4005 |\n"
             "| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(double loose:{3.0,6.0,9.0,12.0}) {
    const auto trace=dm::algorithms::canopy_clustering_trace(points,loose,2.5);
    const auto coverage=dm::algorithms::canopy_coverage(points.size(),trace.canopies);
    std::vector<std::size_t> seeds;for(const auto& canopy:trace.canopies) seeds.push_back(canopy.center);
    if(seeds!=std::vector<std::size_t>{0,30,60} || trace.distance_evaluations!=270) return 1;
    std::print("| {:.1f} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",loose,seeds,coverage.total_memberships,
        trace.distance_evaluations,trace.coordinate_differences,coverage.pair_visits,coverage.shared_pairs.size());
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| T1 | actual seed ids | memberships | distance checks | coordinate differences | pair visits | unique pairs / 4005 |
| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 3.0 | [0, 30, 60] | 90 | 270 | 540 | 1305 | 1305 |
| 6.0 | [0, 30, 60] | 90 | 270 | 540 | 1305 | 1305 |
| 9.0 | [0, 30, 60] | 90 | 270 | 540 | 1305 | 1305 |
| 12.0 | [0, 30, 60] | 270 | 270 | 540 | 12015 | 4005 |
\image(figures/canopy_clusters.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original ninety-point figure and threshold experiment, with actual seeds marked. The table distinguishes repeated memberships, pair visits and unique retained pairs.)
\alttext(Three small rectangular grids of thirty points each are coloured by their disjoint canopy at loose radius three. Seed points are marked at the lower corners.)

For T1=3,6,9, each group contributes thirty memberships and choose(30,2)=435
unique pairs: 1,305 candidates out of 4,005 possible pairs. At T1=12, every
canopy includes all ninety points. There are 270 memberships and 12,015 pair
visits, but only 4,005 unique pairs. Triple-counting the same pairs would
misrepresent the amount of candidate reduction. Construction still makes 270
Euclidean distance evaluations in every row because the seeds remain fixed.

> Close-pair counterexample and a sufficient metric condition

Use point ids 0,1,2,3 at coordinates 0,2,7/8,9/8, in that input order.
With T2=1 and T1=9/8, seed zero consumes point two and seed one consumes
point three. The loose tests are strict: each opposite near point lies exactly
9/8 from the other seed and is excluded. Thus canopies are {0,2} and {1,3}.
Points two and three are only 1/4 apart, below T2, yet share no canopy.
This directly disproves the old unconditional claim even with T1>T2.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/canopy_counterexample.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::vector<double>> points{{0},{2},{.875},{1.125}};
  const auto canopies=dm::algorithms::canopy_clustering(points,1.125,1);
  const auto coverage=dm::algorithms::canopy_coverage(4,canopies);
  const std::pair<std::size_t,std::size_t> close_pair{2,3};
  const bool retained=std::binary_search(coverage.shared_pairs.begin(),coverage.shared_pairs.end(),close_pair);
  const auto safe=dm::algorithms::canopy_coverage(4,dm::algorithms::canopy_clustering(points,2,1));
  const bool restored=std::binary_search(safe.shared_pairs.begin(),safe.shared_pairs.end(),close_pair);
  if(retained || !restored || coverage.shared_pairs.size()!=2 || safe.shared_pairs.size()!=5) return 1;
  std::print("At T1=1.125, T2=1:\n");
  for(const auto& c:canopies) std::print("Seed id {}, x={}: members {}.\n",c.center,points[c.center][0],c.members);
  std::print("Close pair (2,3), distance 0.25, retained: {}. At T1=2, retained: {}.\n",retained,restored);
  std::print("Unique pairs: {} -> {}; pair visits at T1=2: {}.\n",coverage.shared_pairs.size(),safe.shared_pairs.size(),safe.pair_visits);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(70,1050);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle seed;seed.width=4;seed.height=1.8;
  for(std::size_t id=0;id<2;++id) diagram.node(std::format("seed{}",id),{static_cast<double>(id)*8,0},
      std::format("seed {} at x={}\nmembers {}",canopies[id].center,points[canopies[id].center][0],canopies[id].members),seed);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle point;point.width=3;point.height=1.4;point.fill={254,226,226};
  diagram.node("u",{0,-3},"point 2\nx=0.875",point).node("v",{8,-3},"point 3\nx=1.125",point)
      .edge("seed0","u","consumes").edge("seed1","v","consumes");
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke missing;missing.color={220,38,38};missing.dashed=true;
  diagram.path({{1.5,-3},{6.5,-3}},missing).text({4,-2.6},"close pair omitted",18)
      .text({4,2},"strict loose radius 1.125; tight radius 1",20)
      .text({4,-4.6},"distance 0.25 < T2 does not imply shared membership",19)
      .save_svg("build/canopy_counterexample.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
At T1=1.125, T2=1:
Seed id 0, x=0: members [0, 2].
Seed id 1, x=2: members [1, 3].
Close pair (2,3), distance 0.25, retained: false. At T1=2, retained: true.
Unique pairs: 2 -> 5; pair visits at T1=2: 6.
\image(figures/canopy_counterexample.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A close pair can be omitted even though every individual point belongs to a canopy. Increasing the loose radius to twice the tight radius retains this pair.)
\alttext(Two seeds consume one nearby point each into separate canopies. A dashed red connection marks the omitted quarter-unit pair between those points.)

A sufficient condition follows from the triangle inequality. Let r>0 be a
pair distance we want to protect in the same metric. Any point u is either a
selected seed or is consumed by a seed c at distance less than T2, so in either
case d(c,u)<=T2. For d(u,v)<r,

$$
d(c,v)\le d(c,u)+d(u,v)<T2+r.
$$
\alttext(The distance from seed c to v is at most the sum of distances c to u and u to v, which is less than the tight radius plus the desired protected-pair radius.)

If T1>=T2+r, both u and v therefore belong to c's canopy. In particular,
T1>=2*T2 protects pairs strictly closer than positive T2. This is a sufficient
exact-metric condition, not a necessary one and not a guarantee for an unrelated
expensive metric. Floating-point tests near a radius still depend on rounded
norms. The example's binary-exact coordinates make its boundary decisions
hand-checkable.

> Boundaries, work and order-dependent tight-radius effects

T1 must be finite and positive; T2 finite with 0<=T2<=T1. Equal radii are
allowed. At T2=0 each point still consumes itself by selection, but no distance
passes the tight test; duplicate coordinates therefore each become seeds. A
zero-width coordinate row has distance zero to every other zero-width row.
Ragged or nonfinite input is rejected before coordinate access. Hypot accumulation
avoids squaring huge/tiny differences, while unrepresentable distances may still
be infinity and remain outside every allowed finite radius.

Increasing T2 does not universally reduce this greedy algorithm's seed count:
it can remove an earlier useful seed and expose several later ones. The final
program gives a concrete two-to-three increase, alongside exact budgets and
numeric-boundary checks. Input-order dependence is a property of this seed rule.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const std::vector<std::vector<double>> points{{0,0},{2,0},{2.5,1.5},{2.5,-1.5}};
  const auto small=dm::algorithms::canopy_clustering(points,4,1.625),large=dm::algorithms::canopy_clustering(points,4,2.25);
  if(small.size()!=2 || large.size()!=3) return 1;
  std::print("Fixed T1=4: T2=1.625 gives {} seeds; T2=2.25 gives {} seeds.\n",small.size(),large.size());
  const auto zero=dm::algorithms::canopy_clustering_trace({{0},{0}},1,0);
  std::print("Duplicate points at T2=0: {} seeds; first seed newly consumed {}, tight test {}.\n",zero.canopies.size(),
      zero.visits[0].newly_removed,zero.visits[0].within_tight);
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::canopy_clustering_trace({{0},{1},{2},{3}},2.5,1.5,8);
  bool distance_cap=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::canopy_clustering_trace({{0},{1},{2},{3}},2.5,1.5,7);}
  catch(const std::length_error&) {distance_cap=true;}
  bool pair_cap=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::canopy_coverage(4,trace.canopies,8);}
  catch(const std::length_error&) {pair_cap=true;}
  const auto exact=dm::algorithms::canopy_coverage(4,trace.canopies,9);
  bool invalid=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::canopy_clustering({{0},{1,2}},1,1);}
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {invalid=true;}
  const auto huge=dm::algorithms::canopy_clustering({{0},{1e200}},1.1e200,1.1e200);
  const auto tiny=dm::algorithms::canopy_clustering({{0},{1e-200}},.5e-200,.5e-200);
  if(!distance_cap || !pair_cap || !invalid || huge.size()!=1 || tiny.size()!=2) return 1;
  std::print("Exact budgets: {} distance checks, {} pair visits. One-short budgets rejected: {}, {}.\n",
      trace.distance_evaluations,exact.pair_visits,distance_cap,pair_cap);
  std::print("Ragged rows rejected: {}; huge-coordinate seeds: {}; tiny-coordinate seeds: {}.\n",invalid,huge.size(),tiny.size());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Fixed T1=4: T2=1.625 gives 2 seeds; T2=2.25 gives 3 seeds.
Duplicate points at T2=0: 2 seeds; first seed newly consumed true, tight test false.
Exact budgets: 8 distance checks, 9 pair visits. One-short budgets rejected: true, true.
Ragged rows rejected: true; huge-coordinate seeds: 1; tiny-coordinate seeds: 2.
// result_end

With m seeds, this implementation performs exactly n*m Euclidean norm evaluations
and n*m*d coordinate differences, plus membership/eligibility work. Hence worst-case
construction is O(n*n*(d+1)), and ordinary storage is O(n+M) for M memberships.
Tracing stores each actual distance decision, adding O(n*m) records. It performs
no replay. Positive trace budgets are whole-call caps checked before an excess
distance; exceptions return no partial result and never mutate inputs.

The coverage diagnostic separately visits V=sum choose(membership size,2) pairs,
deduplicates into U unique pairs with an ordered set, and stores inverted point
memberships. Its work includes O(V*log(U+1)) plus per-canopy member sorting;
storage is O(n+M+U). A pair budget counts before deduplication and throws before
an excess visit. This explicit diagnostic cost should not be confused with the
cost of a production spatial index or a restricted downstream clusterer.

Native verification compares 6,825 exhaustive ordered small configurations and
1,000 random multidimensional cases with a separate live-list implementation.
An independent all-pairs membership oracle checks unique pairs and duplicate
visit counts. Five hundred further random inputs/orders check the sufficient
metric condition. Tests also cover both counterexamples, exact radii, zero tight
radius/dimension, original grid coordinates, seed/pair monotonicity for fixed T2,
input validation, huge/tiny norms, ownership and whole-call budget boundaries.

> References

- A. McCallum, K. Nigam and L. H. Ungar (2000). Efficient Clustering of High-Dimensional Data Sets with Application to Reference Matching. KDD, 169-178. [Original paper](https://doi.org/10.1145/347090.347123).
- A. McCallum. [Author's presentation description](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~reinf/web/talks-2001/01-03-19.mccallum.html). The two-stage approach and its conditional accuracy claim.
- R Core Team. [Edgar Anderson's Iris Data](https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/datasets/html/iris.html). Measurement units, species structure and provenance.
